Scott's Theorem 7.2: uncurrying the curry of an approximable map h from a product A × B into C returns h, that is, h equals application composed with the pairing of the curried map after the first projection and the second projection.

Extensionality of relations is used. In one direction a witness of the uncurried relation supplies a middle product set, a curry relation after the first projection, a second-projection relation, and an application entailment. The tokens of that entailment give pointwise relations of h, which the union lemma assembles into a relation on a lifted pair. Entailment along the two components of the source, then entailment from the source to the lifted pair of those components, and monotonicity of h, yield the original relation. In the other direction the second component of the source and the output are packaged as a function token. The lifted pair of the two components entails the source, so monotonicity gives a relation of h on that lifted pair and hence a curry relation on the singleton of the token. That relation extends across the first component of the lifted pair of the singleton with the second component, the bottom token being sent to the empty relation. The two projection relations and the application relation, with reflexive input entailment and output entailment by Proposition 2.3(iii), witness the uncurried relation.
